# WAIO Guardian SSH management allowlist -- 750号機's sshd hardening layer.
# format: IP|LABEL
#   IP    - exact management client address (no wildcards, no CIDR)
#   LABEL - free-form description (documentation only)
# lines starting with # and blank lines are ignored.
#
# security/generate_ssh_guardian_config.sh reads this file and renders
# /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/50-waio-guardian.conf's Match Address line from
# it. Every entry here is, by definition, a currently-authorized
# management client -- adding a new admin device (a new laptop, a new
# iPad, a replacement Raspberry Pi SD card with a new address) requires
# an explicit line here, the same way a new egress destination requires
# an explicit line in security/egress_allowlist.conf. No client is ever
# inferred or hardcoded in the generator itself.
#
# This is an example/template (same Public / Private Security Boundary
# pattern as security/egress_allowlist.conf.example, Phase 29). Copy it
# to security/ssh_management_allowlist.conf and fill in this
# deployment's real management client addresses -- see README.md's
# Setup section. The real file is gitignored: if it is missing or empty,
# generate_ssh_guardian_config.sh refuses to generate anything (fails
# closed on the generation step itself), never produces an empty
# Match Address that would deny every management client, and never
# silently falls back to a wide-open config either.
#
# Post-incident note (2026-08-31): this file replaces a single
# hand-written Match Address 192.168.1.91 line that was the sole
# exception to a global PubkeyAuthentication no default. Because only
# 800号機's address was ever added, every other legitimate management
# client (iPad Pro, Raspberry Pi) lost SSH access at the same time
# PasswordAuthentication/KbdInteractiveAuthentication were hardened to
# no. This allowlist plus the generator's lockout-detection check exist
# so that class of bug can't happen silently again.
REPLACE_WITH_HOST800_IP|800号機
REPLACE_WITH_IPAD_PRO_IP|iPad Pro
REPLACE_WITH_RPI_IP|Raspberry Pi
